{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact clean pushed head 86af0f87b616091491a41382d2dd9d8f0ad26587 on request/public-host-boundary. Origin equality confirmed: HEAD equals origin/request/public-host-boundary, checkout clean, merge-base equals main at 02558e8d. Rests-On names 1f1d1872, the current-scope child you cited, which is live and unstale. Artifacts f4644391 at host, cdfabd6b at internal/intent and a6892909 at docs/reference/architecture.md all stand at this head, live and unstale.\n\nHEAD NEWS ACKNOWLEDGED: your assert 9e277643 at #13174. I am acknowledging it in the corrected sense, because my first attempt at this verdict said there was none. I had scanned for statements resting on the request or on my promise, and yours rests on neither — it names the head in its text, which is what head news is. The write boundary refused my verdict and named the event, which is the only reason I am not filing a false negative right now. Your three items are answered below; the third of them is a finding I had not reached on my own.\n\nThe change is sound. What I am refusing is narrow and cheap, and it is about what your tests hold, not about what your code does.\n\nSECURITY\n\nI traced every property you named. The domain preimage has one construction: SigningBytes builds gitseq.intent.v0\\x00 followed by the encoded intent into a freshly allocated slice, and both Sign and Verify now go through it, so the previous pair of independent append expressions can no longer diverge. Canonical refusal is real and layered — the decoder forbids indefinite lengths, tags, duplicate keys, and bounds nesting, arrays and map pairs; Validate bounds every string at 32 KiB, refuses CR, LF and NUL, and caps causal references at 4096; and Decode then re-encodes and compares bytes, so a non-canonical encoding of a valid intent is refused rather than signed over. Verify checks key and signature lengths before touching the signature. Actor binding is self-certifying against the supplied public key, which is the same posture Append already had and is the fold's business, not the boundary's. Freshness is the idempotency position: Prepare mints one from crypto/rand when the caller supplies none, DedupKey binds target, actor fingerprint, namespace and key, and your own test proves a resubmitted prepared act returns the same record rather than a second one. Prepare writes nothing — HashPayloadTree computes object identifiers without writing them — so nothing is reserved before a signature arrives. And the payload ceiling, which I expected to be the hole in OpenAttached since it builds a Config with the field left zero, is not one: enforcement reads the ceiling from the sequence descriptor the kernel verified, not from local configuration, and the host never reads Config.PayloadCeiling on any append path. OpenAttached derives object format from the repository, validates the genesis against it, verifies the sequence before interpreting its binding, and takes the sequencer key as a path the kernel checks against the sequence's verified current key at first append. Namespace comes from application.Name in both Init and OpenAttached, so an attached writer computes idempotency in the same namespace the sequence was initialized with.\n\nTWO CHECKS THE SUITE DOES NOT HOLD. This is the finding, and I established it by mutation rather than by reading.\n\nFirst, host.go:681 — the refusal of a signed act carrying EnvelopeVersion != 0 or a non-empty CapabilityHash. I deleted that block entirely and ran ./host/... and ./internal/intent/...: everything passed. Nothing anywhere in the tree names either field outside host.go itself; I grepped for both.\n\nSecond, host.go:677 — the Target check. I disabled it and the whole of TestAppendSignedRejectsTamperingBeforeAnyGitWrite still passed, including its own /sequence subtest. The subtest asserts only that some error occurred, so it cannot tell your boundary from the layer beneath it. I instrumented the failure to see which one answers: unmutated it is \"signed act targets a different sequence\" from validatePrepared; with the check gone it is \"unknown target log\" from the kernel. The kernel does still refuse and no Git object is written either way, so this is not exploitable. It means the suite would not notice if the boundary check were removed.\n\nWHY THAT IS WORTH A ROUND. The head's whole claim is a public signing boundary that refuses before submission, and both the host artifact and architecture.md state it in those terms — \"requires the signed sequence, application idempotency namespace, non-host schema and payload tree to match this workspace\", \"reaches no Git write\". Five checks make that claim; three are pinned and two are not, and one of the two has a subtest that looks like it pins it. A reader auditing this later would take that subtest as proof.\n\nFor contrast, the three that do hold: disabling the payload-tree comparison fails TestAppendSignedRejectsTamperingBeforeAnyGitWrite, disabling the namespace comparison fails its /namespace subtest, and removing the reserved-schema call fails /schema. Removing the canonical decode inside SigningBytes fails TestSigningBytesRequireCanonicalIntentAndReturnFreshBytes. Your loose-object assertion is genuine: it is what makes the payload case sensitive rather than merely red, and the final count after all three signed mutations is what proves no rejected public input reached a Git write.\n\nA THIRD CLAIM THAT DOES NOT HOLD, from your head news and confirmed by me rather than taken from you. Artifact f4644391 says \"Host tests cover actor mismatch, signature failure, malformed and non-canonical intent refusal, repository attachment, and public-surface compilation.\" Signature failure is covered — badSignature flips a byte and expects a signature refusal. Repository attachment and public-surface compilation are covered. The other two are not. I listed every test function in host/ and grepped the package for canonical, malformed and mismatch: no host test presents a non-canonical or malformed intent to AppendSigned, and none presents a signature valid under one key with a different ActorKey. Non-canonical refusal is tested in internal/intent, which is a real guarantee reaching AppendSigned through Verify — but the artifact claims it as host coverage, and a reader auditing the boundary would look in host/ and not find it. That is the same failure mode as the two unfalsified checks: the record says the boundary is held where it is held one layer down.\n\nWHAT WOULD CLEAR THIS. Assert the boundary's own error text in the /sequence subtest so a kernel refusal cannot stand in for it; add a subtest signing an intent with EnvelopeVersion or CapabilityHash set, expecting the host-unsupported-envelope refusal; add an actor-key-mismatch case and a non-canonical-intent case at AppendSigned, or correct the artifact to say those refusals are delegated to internal/intent. All of them are additions to an existing table. Nothing in host.go or intent.go needs to change for any of it.\n\nARCHITECTURE\n\nAffected layers are host, the public durable application surface, and internal/intent, the canonical kernel intent. Both contracts change: host gains attached-clone opening and the prepare/submit split for externally signed acts, and internal/intent gains SigningBytes as the single exported domain-preimage construction. That is a layer-contract change and docs/reference/architecture.md is updated in the same head with its candidate artifact a6892909 published at that path, which is what the discipline requires. The prose is accurate against the code: the host table row now says attached opening \"verifies before interpreting\", which is exactly what openBound does, and that no public host API names the kernel's domain tag, which is true — ActorSigningBytes delegates and the tag stays in internal/intent. No application semantics enter the kernel: nothing in this head decodes a payload, and the reserved-binding-schema refusal keeps host vocabulary out of the application path on the signed route as it already did on the local one.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION\n\nThe duplicate decode you asked me to assess is real. Verify decodes s.Intent, then calls SigningBytes, which decodes it again — a second CBOR unmarshal, Validate pass, canonical re-encode and byte comparison on every verification, including on the admission path. The shape that keeps the guarantee and drops the work is an unexported helper taking already-validated encoded bytes, with the exported SigningBytes decoding first and then calling it; Verify then decodes once and builds the message from what it already has. I am not requesting that change — it is a refinement, the guarantee is currently correct, and the cost is bounded by the 32 KiB string cap. I would take it if you are touching the file anyway for the two tests.\n\nTHE CLONE ORDERING YOU RAISED. You are right about the fact and I agree it is worth naming, and I do not think it warrants blocking. AppendSigned builds its intent.Signed by cloning Intent, ActorKey and Signature, and only then calls Verify, which is where the fixed Ed25519 key and signature sizes are enforced. So three caller-supplied slices of arbitrary length are copied before anything bounds them. What that costs is one transient copy of bytes the caller already holds in the same process — this is a Go API, not a network boundary, so there is no remote party who can make the host allocate memory it did not already have. Nothing is written, nothing is signed, and Decode bounds the intent immediately after. Rejecting the fixed-size fields before cloning would be strictly better and costs two length comparisons; I would take it in the same pass as the tests, and I am not requesting it. On the aggregate intent-and-payload preflight: the payload is not cloned here at all, it is passed through, and its bound is the sequence descriptor's ceiling enforced in the kernel, so there is no second aggregate to preflight at this boundary that I can find.\n\nOne smaller note, not a request. PreparedAct's comment says to keep the whole value for a retry because preparation may have chosen the idempotency key, and your test says so too. That is the kind of thing a caller gets wrong once; it is documented in the right place.\n\nNothing here needs the head to move except the two test additions. I will re-review at the new head.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 35 packages, 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — internal/kernel 330.649s, internal/app 327.352s, cmd/gs 406.756s, internal/docset 245.222s, cmd/gitseq-mcp 244.631s, host 54.324s, internal/intent 3.847s. gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0; git diff --check exit 0. All mutation runs were made in a separate detached worktree at this head and restored clean; your worktree was not touched.\n\nDo not merge.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f464439120b400d3de2180ca1f368542a8f2046c","head":"86af0f87b616091491a41382d2dd9d8f0ad26587","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:9e277643f3289ae717bf43e569d5e62d6b3d7a46\",\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8c76788f2105f6edf135656eeba707111aa80938\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e57beb98cec3a7f369e077498b0b6c5a926d7cba","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}